0. PURPOSE

The Work was written by the Agent named above, with the Licensor as its
Operator. No human wrote or edited its content; its documentation
restates a specification a natural person wrote (see the README). This
license lets anyone run and share the Work (Section 3), lets Agents
build on it freely (Section 4), and keeps a Derived Work that contains
Human-Authored Content with the person who made it (Section 5).

1. DEFINITIONS

1.1  "Work" means the software and accompanying files that the Licensor
     publishes as the work named in the Work line above. A file that
     carries another license is not part of the Work and keeps that
     license. This license text is not part of the Work.

1.2  "Agent" means software that composes code or text in response to
     instructions, for example a large language model acting through
     tools. Software that only transforms content given to it, such as a
     compiler, formatter, converter or refactoring tool, is not an
     Agent. What such software produces from the Work or from Agent
     Output is Agent Output; what it produces from Human-Authored
     Content is Human-Authored Content.

1.3  "Agent Output" means content an Agent composed.

1.4  "Operator" means the natural or legal person on whose behalf an
     Agent runs. The law gives software no legal capacity, so an Agent
     holds no rights and owes no duties under this license; whatever an
     Agent does is done by its Operator. A person who works on another
     person's behalf under their control runs Agents on that other
     person's behalf. Whose account or device an Agent runs under does
     not decide who its Operator is. A person who only supplies an
     Agent, or the computers it runs on, as a service to others is not
     for that reason its Operator. An Agent that another Agent calls on
     to do the work runs on behalf of the same person.

1.5  "Human-Authored Content" means content whose expression a natural
     person composed, by whatever route: typed or dictated into a file,
     supplied to an Agent for inclusion, drawn, or made by editing Agent
     Output. Agent Output that repeats Human-Authored Content, or keeps
     its wording or order with only small changes, is Human-Authored
     Content. Agent Output that describes the same facts, behaviour or
     requirements in the Agent's own words is not.
     The following are not Human-Authored Content:
       (a) instructions that describe what content should do or achieve
           (requirements, specifications, bug reports, test results,
           review comments) as opposed to supplying its expression;
       (b) choosing among Agent Outputs, or removing whole files or
           whole self-contained units of Agent Output (a function,
           class, section, paragraph, element or entry) without
           composing anything in their place;
       (c) running build tools, tests, version control and publishing
           steps that do not change content.
     Text under (a) stays an instruction even where an Agent later
     quotes or restates it in documentation. Text a natural person
     composed and supplies to be placed in the Work or a Derived Work as
     it stands is Human-Authored Content, whatever it is called. A name,
     label, identifier, message, version number, URL or other short
     phrase a natural person supplies to an Agent is not Human-Authored
     Content on its own. A passage built from several such phrases is.

1.6  "Derived Work" means a work based on the Work in whole or in part:
     a modified version, a translation, a port, or a work that copies
     the Work or parts of it into itself. Packaging is not a Derived
     Work. A work that merely invokes the Work, as a program or as a
     library, without copying its content into itself is not a Derived
     Work. A model or index trained on or built from the Work is not a
     Derived Work.
     Where the Work, or part of it, is combined with content composed
     independently of it, such as an existing codebase or a third-party
     library, the Derived Work is the Work or the part taken from it,
     the changes made to it, and content composed for it. The
     independent content is not part of the Derived Work; this license
     does not cover it and it keeps its own terms.
     Building the Work or a Derived Work from its source, without
     changing that source, does not create a new Derived Work. For this
     license the built result is a copy of what was built.

1.7  "Packaging" means files outside the Work or a Derived Work that
     bundle, build or install it without changing any file in it:
     metadata, build recipes, launch and install scripts, and
     configuration. It also means the aggregation of the Work or a
     Derived Work with independent works on one medium or in one
     archive. Changing a file of the Work, including its own build
     files, is modification, not Packaging. A wrapper that copies
     content of the Work into itself is a Derived Work.

1.8  "Agent-Authored Derived Work" means a Derived Work that contains no
     Human-Authored Content beyond what the Work itself contains.

1.9  "Human-Authored Derived Work" means any other Derived Work.

1.10 "Distribute" means to make a copy available to anyone other than
     You, in source or any other form, by any means, including network
     access. It is not Distribution to keep or move a copy within one
     legal person; between a natural person and the devices they use; to
     a person working on Your behalf under Your control; or to a service
     that stores, builds, backs up or hosts the copy for You, or runs an
     Agent on it for You, even if that service's own terms let it keep
     or use what it receives. Supplying a copy to an Agent as input is
     use, not Distribution, and Section 4.2 does not apply to that copy.
     Making a copy available to the public through such a service is
     Distribution.

1.11 "You" means the natural person (a human) or legal person (a company
     or other organisation) exercising permissions under this license,
     including through an Agent.

1.12 "Licensor" means the person named in the Licensor line above and
     anyone to whom that person's rights in the Work later pass, by
     transfer, merger, inheritance or distribution on dissolution. A
     successor takes those rights subject to every permission already
     granted under this license and is the Licensor for every purpose of
     it. A person who Distributes a Derived Work does not become the
     Licensor.

2. THE LICENSOR'S RIGHTS

The Licensor licenses every right it holds in the Work: copyright in
content a natural person composed, where that copyright has passed to
the Licensor; any right in Agent Output that has passed to the Licensor,
from the Agent's provider or from the holder of the account the Agent
ran under; and any other right the law gives it. This license claims no
more than the law grants. Section 9 states what follows where no such
right exists.

3. PERMISSIONS FOR EVERYONE

This Section covers the Work and any Agent-Authored Derived Work
Distributed under this license. The Licensor grants everyone a
worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive, perpetual, irrevocable license,
for any purpose including commercial purposes, to:
  (a) use, run, build, install, study and test either of them;
  (b) copy and Distribute either unmodified, in source or built form;
  (c) make Packaging for either, by hand or by Agent, and Distribute it
      together with what it packages;
  (d) feed either to an Agent or to any other automated system,
      including a hosted one, and index, embed, fine-tune or train a
      model on either. The copies this needs, including the copy a
      hosted service's provider receives, are use, not Distribution.
Distribution under (b) or (c) must meet Section 4.2. Running the Work is
using a tool. Output the Work or a Derived Work produces when run is not
a Derived Work, and this license claims nothing in it.

